<div class="article-body">

<p>How does China's AI training stack up globally?</p>

<p>According to McKinsey's newly released <em>HR Monitor 2026</em> (covering 10 countries, 1,300+ HR professionals and 5,500 employees), the numbers are striking:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>49% of employees received formal Gen AI training</strong> — compared to 25% in Europe and 31% in the U.S. (which dropped 14 percentage points year-over-year)</li>
  <li><strong>77% use AI tools daily</strong> — compared to 47% in the U.S. and 36% in Europe</li>
  <li><strong>Only 9% received zero training in the past year</strong> — compared to 24% in Europe, with Netherlands and Germany at 35%</li>
</ul>

<p>By these metrics alone, China looks like a clear global leader in AI talent development.</p>

<p>But the same McKinsey report reveals another set of numbers:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Only <strong>43% of Chinese HR departments have operational AI deployment</strong> (global average: 28%)</li>
  <li>Globally, 37% of HR AI initiatives remain in pilot phase, and 35% are still in the exploratory stage</li>
  <li><strong>34% of European HR professionals "don't know what Agentic AI is"</strong></li>
  <li>HR professionals' perception of AI's automation potential has actually <strong>declined</strong> — from estimating that "over 30% of HR work can be automated" in 2025 to just 20% in 2026</li>
  <li>32% of organizations still operate under the classic Ulrich HR model</li>
</ul>

<p>This creates a revealing tension:</p>

<p><strong>Chinese companies lead the world in getting employees to use AI — but the very HR departments that should orchestrate this transformation are only just beginning their own AI journeys.</strong></p>

<hr />

<h2>1. The Reality Behind China's AI Training Leadership</h2>

<h3>1.1 49% Formal Training: A Dual Engine of Policy and Market Demand</h3>

<p>China's 49% formal training rate is nearly double Europe's 25%, and 18 percentage points ahead of the U.S. This gap comes from two engines running simultaneously.</p>

<p><strong>Policy-driven (approximately 40-50%).</strong> Between 2024 and 2025, China issued a series of AI talent policies — the Ministry of Education's "AI-Empowered Education" initiative and the Ministry of Human Resources' AI skills training subsidy program. Large state-owned enterprises (especially in finance, energy, and telecommunications) were required to conduct full AI literacy training for all employees, directly lifting national coverage rates.</p>

<p><strong>Enterprise-driven (approximately 50-60%).</strong> Chinese companies — particularly in internet, manufacturing, and retail — have been among the fastest globally in deploying AI applications. They've already seen tangible efficiency gains, creating real demand for further training. Domestic large language models (DeepSeek, Tongyi, Wenxin, etc.) are accessible via PC and mobile with virtually no hardware barrier.</p>

<p>This is a textbook case of "policy builds the stage, enterprises perform the show." But a caveat: <strong>"training coverage" does not equal "training effectiveness."</strong> The survey's "formal training" definition may include sessions as short as one hour. The proportion of employees completing 20+ hours of hands-on AI training is likely far lower than 49%.</p>

<h3>1.2 77% Daily Use: Tool-Level Acceptance ≠ Capability-Level Depth</h3>

<p>Three out of four Chinese employees use AI every day — but the question is what they're using it for.</p>

<p>Chinese users' primary AI use cases cluster around "substitutive use": writing emails, creating presentations, searching for information. True "augmentative use" — embedding AI into core workflows, changing decision-making processes — likely represents a much smaller share.</p>

<p><strong>77% signals "tool-level acceptance," not "capability-level depth."</strong> The former answers "Can they open it?", the latter asks "Can they use it better?"</p>

<blockquote>
<p>An interesting comparison: U.S. daily AI usage dropped from 64% to 47% (year-over-year -17pp). Likely causes include a "hype normalization" effect — early-2024 peak experimentation gave way to more selective, value-driven usage by 2026. China's 77% hasn't declined yet. Whether this reflects "more solid use cases" or "still in the first wave" remains to be seen.</p>
</blockquote>

<h3>1.3 Just 9% Zero-Training: Institutional Advantage or Institutional Dependency?</h3>

<p>China's 9% vs. the Netherlands/Germany's 35% is one of the report's most striking contrasts. This is more likely an institutional difference than a development-stage gap: China operates a "centralized training model" where large companies have annual training plans with KPIs, distributing resources top-down. Europe's "decentralized training model" leaves more to individual initiative and employer discretion.</p>

<p>Each model has its own risks. China's high coverage comes with a risk of "box-ticking." McKinsey's report identifies a "training illusion" — HR departments estimate 6.2 training days per employee per year, while the actual figure is 3.4 days. Behind China's 49% training coverage rate, a similar illusion may be hiding.</p>

<p><strong>Module 1 conclusion: China's AI training "quantity" leadership is real, but it's a "volume lead" rather than a "quality lead."</strong> Chinese companies have completed the first step — getting everyone to use AI. The second step — turning AI into a core organizational capability — is just beginning.</p>

<hr />

<h2>2. Why Is HR's Own AI Transformation So Slow?</h2>

<p>Global HR AI operational deployment stands at just 28%. China's 43% is higher, but still means over half of HR functions haven't reached operational deployment. Why is HR consistently among the slowest functions in AI adoption?</p>

<h3>2.1 Organizational Inertia: HR's Core Work Is Naturally "Anti-Automation"</h3>

<p>The core of HR work — talent judgment, organizational design, culture-building — is high-touch by nature. HR professionals genuinely believe the "human touch" is irreplaceable. The data confirms this: HR professionals' estimate of AI's automation potential dropped from "over 30%" in 2025 to just 20% in 2026.</p>

<p>This "perception decline" tells a story. The most likely explanation: the "over-optimism → execution setback → expectation correction" cycle. In 2024-2025, HR departments invested heavily in AI recruitment, AI interviewing, and AI performance evaluation tools — only to find implementation harder than expected. AI interviews were criticized as "impersonal." AI performance reviews were seen as unfair. Employee churn prediction models had low accuracy. Reality proved more complex than the hype suggested.</p>

<h3>2.2 Talent Structure: HR Departments Lack AI-Literate Professionals</h3>

<p>This may be the most straightforward barrier. Among Chinese HR practitioners over 40, the proportion with digital literacy is far lower than in IT, product, or marketing functions. Digital literacy's jump from 20th to 6th in skill demand rankings over three years shows how fast the market is moving — but existing talent's skill transition takes time.</p>

<p>Even when AI tools are available, HR departments often lack the technical judgment to evaluate, select, and deploy them. A common pattern: HR identifies a need, IT evaluates and deploys a tool, but neither department fully understands the other's requirements for "AI in HR."</p>

<h3>2.3 KPI Misalignment: HR's Metrics Don't Directly Benefit from AI</h3>

<p>IT departments pursue efficiency (AI's cost-reduction impact is directly measurable). Sales teams pursue revenue growth (AI's conversion rate lift is directly measurable). But HR's core KPIs — hiring quality, employee satisfaction, organizational health — have no straightforward link to AI tool adoption. Senior leadership cares about "employee well-being" and "team stability," not "how many AI tools HR is using." Without direct accountability, urgency to self-transform stays low.</p>

<h3>2.4 Organizational Models Haven't Caught Up</h3>

<p>32% of organizations still operate under the classic Ulrich three-pillar model (HRBP + COE + SSC). This model was designed for the industrial era — its core logic is "specialization + scale." AI-era HR operations need "data-driven agility + cross-functional collaboration."</p>

<p><strong>An organization still executing its Ulrich transformation cannot simultaneously complete its AI transformation.</strong></p>

<p>Ulrich is fundamentally a "standardization" mindset, at odds with AI's "dynamic adaptation" logic. While HR departments debate "should BP roles be full-function or not," AI technology is already demanding capabilities in data engineering, prompt design, and workflow automation from HR teams.</p>

<hr />

<h2>3. The Core Tension: What "Leading in Training but Lagging in Self-Transformation" Really Means</h2>

<p>This is the article's central question.</p>

<p>McKinsey's report defines a <strong>"dual role" for HR in the AI era</strong>:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>The Architect role</strong>: Design the enterprise's AI-era talent management system — skill taxonomy rebuilding, training system design, performance evaluation updates, organizational restructuring</li>
  <li><strong>The Lighthouse role</strong>: Lead by example — demonstrate how AI transforms work by embedding it into HR's own processes first</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Which role fails first if HR lags behind?</strong></p>

<p>The Lighthouse role failing only means HR's own efficiency suffers. But the Architect role requires deep AI understanding to design effective systems: an HR professional who has never used AI recruiting tools cannot design effective AI-powered recruitment processes; one who has never used AI to optimize learning cannot design effective AI-era training curricula.</p>

<p>Losing the Architect role risks downgrading HR from "strategic partner" back to "administrative executor" — unable to answer the CEO's question "What's our AI talent strategy?" with a data-driven plan.</p>

<h3>The Efficiency Paradox: The Longer HR Waits, the Harder It Gets</h3>

<p>A troubling cycle emerges:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>Company accelerates AI deployment → needs HR to redesign talent systems → HR's AI capability is insufficient → company bypasses HR, IT deploys AI tools directly → HR becomes marginalized → its capability drops further → it falls even further behind.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>This cycle is already manifesting in Chinese companies. In many organizations, HR became a "bystander" to AI deployment — IT pushed tools directly, and HR was informed afterward to play catch-up.</p>

<p><strong>But this is also China's HR opportunity.</strong> Because Chinese companies are moving fast on AI, the "service window" for HR is shorter — but if HR can accelerate within this window, the payoff far exceeds that of slower competitors still watching.</p>

<hr />

<h2>4. Three Recommendations for Chinese Enterprise HR Leaders</h2>

<h3>Recommendation 1: Be the Lighthouse Before the Architect</h3>

<p>HR's own AI transformation should become a visible demonstration project. Start with 2-3 urgent, high-ROI scenarios: recruitment (AI resume screening + interview assistance), learning and development (AI-personalized learning paths), and performance management (AI-assisted goal tracking). Quantify and showcase results — "recruitment efficiency up X%," "training completion rate up Y%" — then roll out across the company.</p>

<p>Key principle: <strong>Don't wait until HR fully understands AI to start.</strong> Let results do the persuasion.</p>

<h3>Recommendation 2: Upgrade from "Training Organizer" to "AI Transformation Architect"</h3>

<p>This isn't a title change — it's a fundamental shift in operating logic:</p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Old Role: Training Organizer</th>
    <th>New Role: AI Transformation Architect</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Employees need X → run a course on X</td>
    <td>Analyze AI capability data → design targeted training</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Training delivered = success</td>
    <td>Training translated into behavior change = success</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Annual training plan</td>
    <td>Continuous AI learning system</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Buy external courses</td>
    <td>Build internal contextualized learning</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Training is "extra work"</td>
    <td>Training embedded into daily workflow</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p>A concrete entry point: digital literacy's jump from 20th to 6th in skill demand rankings means CEOs and business leaders already feel the urgency. HR must leverage this moment to redesign the training system, not just add a few AI courses.</p>

<h3>Recommendation 3: Link Compensation Incentives to AI Capability Building</h3>

<p>McKinsey's report has one more critical data point for HR leaders: <strong>compensation and benefits became the #1 retention factor (52%, up 24 percentage points year-over-year).</strong> In the face of AI-driven career uncertainty, employees' first instinct is not "learn more AI skills" but "secure my income first."</p>

<p>If HR invests heavily in AI training while ignoring compensation competitiveness, the likely employee response is: "Training is great, but my paycheck hasn't changed."</p>

<p><strong>AI training needs to be tied to compensation and promotion</strong>: complete an AI certification → salary increase or promotion. This chain must work. Otherwise, training remains a one-directional gesture of goodwill, not a mutual value exchange.</p>

<hr />

<h2>Closing Thoughts</h2>

<blockquote>
<p>China's AI training leadership is real, but it should not be a source of complacency. The 49% training rate and 77% daily usage are genuinely impressive numbers. But the real determinant of whether AI graduates from "productivity tool" to "core capability" in Chinese enterprises lies in whether HR departments can accelerate their own transformation.</p>
<p>If companies want AI to truly reshape their competitiveness, HR must play both roles simultaneously: prove AI can change how work gets done (the lighthouse) and build the systems that enable the entire organization to thrive in the AI era (the architect). One is "doing it yourself," the other is "leading the organization to do it." Both are essential.</p>
</blockquote>

